    "body": "- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\r\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n\r\nAdded a feature to persist bookmarks to disk so they are saved when you open from the same directory again. Currently they are saved per current working directory and restored upon opening the explorer. \r\n\r\nIf it's possible I would like some suggestions on how to better integrate it with the existing structure of the mini.files plugin (like the history and tabs). If the current approach with the cwd is good enough I would like just the go ahead before I start writing tests.\r\n\r\nTests aren't yet written because currently the way the bookmarks are persisted is subject to change. ",
    "closed_at": "2025-03-02T12:03:37Z",
    "comment_data": [],
    "comment_regular_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the PR!\r\n\r\nI am afraid, this is not quite aligned with the 'mini.files' design (and 'mini.nvim' in general). I'd like to do persistent features only if absolutely necessary for the module's functionality. This results in both more secure and less complex code overall.\r\n\r\nThere is an example of doing a [\"persistent\" set of custom bookmarks](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/f40da50a3dfbbc53e6173beef8dc28626e1b5ebc/doc/mini-files.txt#L537-L552) in a sense that they will be present in any explorer. Extending the example to depend on some kind of path (be it explorer's anchor or cwd) should be straightforward with different code paths inside autocommand's callback.\r\n\r\nClosing as not planned.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nSome tips for the future:\r\n- Please, create a feature request first before doing a PR ( as is suggested in 'CONTRIBUTING.md'). This will save both sides some time.\r\n- Implementing a feature with +163 lines diff while +98 lines are responsible for the custom table serialization helper code is bound to be met with skepticism. Luckily, there is no need for that, as Neovim comes with `:h vim.inspect()`.",
